WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Practical Application of Defect Opportunities

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.366
Welcome to this session on defect opportunities.

3
00:00:08.366 --> 00:00:14.132
Right now, we're looking at a series of construction standards.

4
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:19.399
Let's take a look at those, to understand those, we’re going to zero in here.

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:25.366
We have PD, for possible deficiency, AT, acceptable tolerance,

6
00:00:25.366 --> 00:00:28.632
and CR contractor responsibility.

7
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:35.633
What we are looking at is that portion of the code that is associated with air conditioning.

8
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:38.666
We’ll make that very bright there.

9
00:00:38.666 --> 00:00:41.632
Air Conditioning and Heating.

10
00:00:41.633 --> 00:00:49.599
So, on row 12, we have a potential deficiency called inadequate heat.

11
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.833
I'll bring that up here. Inadequate heat.

12
00:00:54.833 --> 00:01:01.533
Another, is excessive noise from ducts.

13
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:04.566
So, when we’re building a home.

14
00:01:04.566 --> 00:01:09.366
We check when we finish for the adequacy of heat

15
00:01:09.366 --> 00:01:13.266
and how quiet it is from the ducts.

16
00:01:13.266 --> 00:01:17.132
Another one is inadequate combustion air vent.

17
00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:22.933
So here what we’re doing again, is we have a set of standards,

18
00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:26.933
workmanship standards, for the state of Arizona

19
00:01:26.933 --> 00:01:32.766
associated with air conditioning and heating for residential homes.

20
00:01:32.766 --> 00:01:38.066
The first one was in adequate heating. Second is excessive noise from ducts.

21
00:01:38.066 --> 00:01:42.066
And third, is inadequate combustion air vent.

22
00:01:42.066 --> 00:01:47.666
Here's another one. The unit is too close to the door.

23
00:01:47.666 --> 00:01:53.366
So you see, each one of these constitutes a defect opportunity.

24
00:01:53.366 --> 00:01:58.966
We could wind up converting this into Sigma capabilities.

25
00:01:58.966 --> 00:02:03.932
We could develop a Sigma capability for air conditioning and heating.

26
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:08.699
As well as for the entire structure, by pooling them upward.

27
00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:14.600
Again our job here, is to simply look at what constitutes a defect opportunity.

28
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.600
This time we're doing it in the area of construction.

29
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.166
This should be very generalizable to other areas.

30
00:02:23.166 --> 00:02:28.166
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
